# Bytes a rule of Catalan's names by address, which no lifter writes: these
# are ours. The phonemes are the phone statement's own alphabet, which
# tools/lang-phonemes.py caes prints.
#
# Catalan has two affricates Castilian has not. The voiced postalveolar --
# metge, platja, viatge -- and the voiced alveolar -- dotze, setze. Spanish
# declares a code for the first, J, and never reaches it from its own
# spelling, so nothing in the module speaks it: there is no span_ph_J, and no
# arm of the value chain that would expand it the way C is expanded into a
# stop and a fricative. Inserting it laid down a phone nothing could say and
# the word stopped where it stood.
#
# So a letter rule lays down the two phones itself, which is the same two
# sounds in the same order and asks nothing of the module that is not already
# there. That is what Polish did with its own hard affricates for the same
# reason.
bytes cat_ph_dzz 05 12   # d, then the voiced postalveolar
bytes cat_ph_dz  05 0e   # d, then a voiced s

# The schwa, which is code 37 and the module's first phoneme of its own.
# Central Catalan reduces every unstressed a and e to it, and it is the sound
# that makes the language recognisable: casa is not [kasa] but [kaza], and
# the last vowel is neither a nor e.
bytes cat_ph_schwa 25

# The two open vowels, and the two letters that write them.
#
# Catalan has seven stressed vowels where Castilian has five, and the pairs it
# adds are the open e and the open o: sec and sec, dona and dona, are one
# letter apart in writing and one vowel apart in speech. The accent is what
# tells them apart -- the acute is the close vowel and the grave the open one
# -- so the grave letters have to reach a rule of their own rather than being
# flattened to a plain vowel on the way in.
bytes cat_letter_egrave 62   # e with a grave, by its code in the alphabet
bytes cat_letter_ograve 64   # o with a grave
bytes cat_ph_eopen 26        # the open e, phone 38
bytes cat_ph_oopen 27        # the open o, phone 39

# The digits, as Catalan says them. They are characters rather than phonemes:
# a number word goes in as letters and the letter rules then read it, which is
# the same path a letter's own name takes. tools/lang-alphabet.py caes says
# which code is which character.
#
# Castilian says these through span_char_name, a dictionary, and changing a
# word there wants an action whose record is a length that rule already lays
# down -- and even a rewrite that keeps the length comes out silent, because
# what delta-dict.py writes is reached by an address the arena does not know.
# So the digits are laid down by a rule instead, out of bytes of ours, which
# is a store the arena is told about.
#
# Most are spelled with an accent, and the accent does two jobs. A vowel
# that carries one takes the word's stress, which is what puts vintiun's
# stress on the un and vintissis's on the sis, where the stress rule, reading
# the last letter, put both a syllable back. And the accent says which of
# the two mid vowels it is: set is [s E t] and nou [n O w], open, so they
# carry a grave, and dos is [d o s], close, so it carries an acute. On its
# own each digit is one syllable and the accent changes nothing but the
# vowel; in a compound it is the stress.
#
# Two are left plain, because their accented spelling reads wrong here and
# the stress rule already puts them right: quatre ends in a vowel and takes
# the penultimate, cinc ends in a consonant and takes the last, alone and
# after vinti alike. An a with a grave is dropped by the letter rules, so
# quatre with one came out [k w t r e], and a c before an i with an acute is
# read as a k, so cinc with one came out [k i ng k].
#
# un is written hun, because an h says nothing and an i before a u would
# otherwise make the u a glide: vintihun is [b i n t i u n] and not
# [b i n t i w n]. sis and set are written with two esses, because a single s
# between two vowels is voiced in Catalan and here it must not be: vintissis
# is [b i n t i s i s].
bytes cat_num_zero    1f 02 18 04         # zero
bytes cat_num_un      10 6f 15            # hun, u acute
bytes cat_num_dos     0d 6e 19            # dos, o acute
bytes cat_num_tres    1a 18 62 19         # tres, e grave
bytes cat_num_quatre  17 05 01 1a 18 02   # quatre
bytes cat_num_cinc    0c 03 15 0c         # cinc
bytes cat_num_sis     19 19 6d 19         # ssis, i acute
bytes cat_num_set     19 19 62 1a         # sset, e grave
bytes cat_num_vuit    1b 6f 03 1a         # vuit, u acute
bytes cat_num_nou     15 64 05            # nou, o grave

# And the teens, which Catalan makes its own way: ten is deu, and eleven to
# sixteen are one word each where seventeen to nineteen are dis- and di- on
# the front of seven, eight and nine.
bytes cat_num_deu     0d 62 05                  # deu, e grave
bytes cat_num_onze    04 15 1f 02               # onze
bytes cat_num_dotze   0d 04 1a 1f 02            # dotze
bytes cat_num_tretze  1a 18 02 1a 1f 02         # tretze
bytes cat_num_catorze 0c 01 1a 04 18 1f 02      # catorze
bytes cat_num_quinze  17 05 03 15 1f 02         # quinze
bytes cat_num_setze   19 62 1a 1f 02            # setze, e grave
bytes cat_num_disset  0d 03 19 19 62 1a         # disset, e grave
bytes cat_num_divuit  0d 03 1b 05 03 1a         # divuit
bytes cat_num_dinou   0d 03 15 64 05            # dinou, o grave

# Twenty to twenty-nine. Catalan writes vint-i-un as one word with the i in
# it, and it goes in as one word here: the i cannot be a word of its own,
# because the conjunction i is a stressed content word in this module, which
# docs/status.md records, and a stressed i between vint and un is not a
# number anybody says. The unit carries the accent, so the stress goes to it:
# vintihun with the acute is [b i n t i u n] with the un stressed, where
# without it the rule that reads the last letter put the stress on the ti.
# The paragraph above says why the h and the double s.
bytes cat_num_vint         1b 03 15 1a
bytes cat_num_vintiun      1b 03 15 1a 03 10 6f 15
bytes cat_num_vintidos     1b 03 15 1a 03 0d 6e 19
bytes cat_num_vintitres    1b 03 15 1a 03 1a 18 62 19
bytes cat_num_vintiquatre  1b 03 15 1a 03 17 05 01 1a 18 02
bytes cat_num_vinticinc    1b 03 15 1a 03 0c 03 15 0c
bytes cat_num_vintisis     1b 03 15 1a 03 19 19 6d 19
bytes cat_num_vintiset     1b 03 15 1a 03 19 19 62 1a
bytes cat_num_vintivuit    1b 03 15 1a 03 1b 6f 03 1a
bytes cat_num_vintinou     1b 03 15 1a 03 15 64 05

# The tens that fit where Castilian's stand. convert_span_double pushes a
# symbol and inserts a fixed number of characters from it, so a Catalan word
# of the same length goes in its place by pointing the push at bytes of ours.
bytes cat_num_noranta   15 04 18 01 15 1a 01 48      # noranta, padded to eight
bytes cat_num_quaranta  17 05 01 18 01 15 1a 01      # quaranta, for cuarenta
bytes cat_num_cinquanta 0c 03 15 17 05 01 15 1a 01   # cinquanta, for cincuenta

# Three more tens. trenta is padded to the eight characters the arm it shares
# lays down; seixanta wants one more than sesenta did, so its call site is
# widened by a character too; setanta is exactly as long as setenta.
bytes cat_num_trenta   1a 18 02 15 1a 01 48 48      # trenta, padded, for treinta
bytes cat_num_seixanta 19 02 03 1d 01 15 1a 01      # seixanta, for sesenta
bytes cat_num_setanta  19 02 1a 01 15 1a 01 48      # setanta, padded to eight
bytes cat_num_vuitanta 1b 05 03 1a 01 15 1a 01      # vuitanta, for ochenta

# What goes between the pieces of a compound number: a word boundary and a
# space, which are two characters here. c7 is the mark IBM's own insert_space
# lays down in front of every space it makes, and it is the mark and not the
# space that build_words ends a word at -- a space put in on its own was
# walked over as if it were a letter, and quaranta hun stayed one word of
# eleven letters.
#
# Thirty-one to ninety-nine are said as two words, trenta and un, quaranta
# and cinc, because the machine gives a word one stress and reduces every
# other vowel: as one word quarantacinc came out [k w @ r @ n t @ s i ng k]
# with the a of ranta gone to a schwa, and seixantasis put its stress on the
# ta. Said as two words each half keeps its own vowel and its own stress,
# which is what a Catalan compound does -- quaranta-cinc is
# [k w @ r a n t @ s i ng k], with the secondary stress on ranta that the
# hyphen writes. The twenties are one word each and do not come through here.
bytes cat_num_join c7 48

# The d a letter rule lays in front of the voiced postalveolar to make the
# affricate of it. span_ph_Z_dur asks whether one stands before the Z, which
# is how it knows the fricative is the second half of an affricate and gives
# it the short duration.
bytes cat_ph_d 05

# The velar fricative's code, retaken. Catalan has no such sound: j and g
# before e and i are the voiced postalveolar here, so no Catalan spelling
# reaches phone 16 and the whole of the module's machinery for it -- an arm in
# the value chain, an arm in the durations, a record every neighbour reads --
# is standing idle. It is what a Catalan word-final r turns into, and
# ss_val.up says what that then sounds like, which is nothing.
bytes cat_ph_mute 10

# The phone a Catalan word ends an n with. Castilian velarises a word-final n
# and Catalan does not, and the way that was stopped was to spell the final
# one with the interdental's code, which no Catalan spelling can reach and
# which the arm doing the velarising does not test for. st_strss.up is where
# the price of that is paid back: the stress rule asks whether a word ends in
# a nasal by the code as well, so it has to be told about this one too.
bytes cat_ph_final_n 0f

# The voiced postalveolar on its own, which is cat_ph_dzz without the stop in
# front of it. j and g before e and i lay one down or the other, and a rule of
# ours has to name both by address to choose between them: glob.up says which
# is said where.
bytes cat_ph_zz 12

# The glide and the fricative that ix says, as one insertion.
#
# Catalan writes the postalveolar after a vowel as ix, and the i is not only a
# graphic mark: caixa is [ka j S @] and calaix [k @ l a j S], with an audible
# glide that the spelling is there to record. Central Catalan is the dialect
# that drops it and Western and Valencian keep it, so both are Catalan; this
# is the one that can be heard, and hearing it is what tells calaix from a
# word that ends in a bare fricative.
#
# It is two phones from one letter, which is what insert_2pt_s's _4_2 form is
# for and what cat_ph_dzz above already does for the affricates. The glide
# first, then the fricative: y is 29 and S is 17.
bytes cat_ph_ysh 1d 11
